description:
crate-jmap-cid-types/src/digest.rs:44-47:

    NonHexLowercase {
        /// 0-based byte index of the first offending character.
        at: usize,
    },

The variant reports WHERE the offending byte is but not WHAT it is. A consumer that wants to surface a precise diagnostic — e.g. \"sha256 digest has uppercase 'F' at position 17, expected lowercase\" — cannot do so from the error alone. They have to re-walk the original input string to fish out the bad byte:

    Err(err) => {
        if let Sha256DigestErrorKind::NonHexLowercase { at } = err.kind {
            let bad = input.as_bytes()[at]; // re-walk and re-bounds-check
            log::warn!(\"non-hex byte 0x{bad:02x} at position {at}\");
        }
    }

This forces 100% of consumers that want a meaningful diagnostic to write the same boilerplate. The library has the offending byte in hand at construction time (digest.rs:119-125, the validator loop holds `b` and `i` simultaneously) and discards it. Adding a `byte: u8` field would be free in the construction path and would deliver a complete diagnostic to every caller.

Suggested shape (composes with the per-variant non_exhaustive fix in the sibling finding):

    #[non_exhaustive]
    NonHexLowercase {
        at: usize,
        byte: u8,
    },

With per-variant #[non_exhaustive] this is a backward-compatible addition. Without it, adding `byte` is a breaking change in v0.2 — which is why these two findings are linked.

This is api-contract,defensive in the workspace's terms: the API shape forces every caller to write the same boilerplate (re-walking the input to recover the discarded byte) when a small additive change would have done it once in the library.
